1
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:16,040
Nuclear energy, simple in original conception, complex in final achievement.

2
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:27,040
The metallurgy of uranium is less than 20 years old, yet nuclear energy is already being used

3
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:40,040
and accepted in many areas of American life. Over 750 million kilowatt hours of

4
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:48,040
electricity have been generated by atomic power. And now nuclear energy is

5
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,040
entering the space age.

6
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:27,040
Long-life Earth satellites for communications, weather surveillance, and a host of other

7
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:35,040
important space activities must have low weight, compact and rugged electric power sources.

8
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:43,040
Nuclear energy can provide that power reliably and safely.

9
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:48,040
Reactor systems are being designed and built by Atomics International for the United States

10
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:53,040
Atomic Energy Commission as a part of a commission program to develop devices which will generate

11
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:56,040
electricity for at least one year in space.

12
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:05,040
This development program is designated SNAP, S-N-A-P, Systems for Nuclear Auxiliary Power.

13
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:12,040
The program is based on the use of radioisotopes and nuclear fission reactors to generate electricity

14
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:14,040
for space vehicles and remote locations.

15
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:21,040
The SNAP-2 reactor system,

16
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:25,040
under development since 1957 for use by the United States Air Force,

17
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:28,040
will produce 3,000 watts of electricity

18
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:30,040
for space applications beyond 1964.

19
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:37,040
This small, lightweight reactor is coupled to a mercury vapor turbo generator,

20
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:39,040
which has only one moving part.

21
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:48,040
The SNAP-8 system, under joint development by the Atomic Energy Commission and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration,

22
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:57,040
will produce ten times the power output of SNAP-2 and will pioneer electric propulsion in America's space program after 1965.

23
00:02:57,140 --> 00:03:05,040
The SNAP-10A system uses the SNAP-2 reactor

24
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:09,040
and a thermoelectric generator which directly converts heat to electricity

25
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:15,040
with no mechanical moving parts. It is capable of delivering any power below 1,000 watts.

26
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:19,040
The SNAP-10A is intended for use after 1963.

27
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:25,040
The three systems, SNAP-2, 8 and 10A

28
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:31,040
all use small, compact nuclear reactors of identical concept.

29
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:35,040
This full-scale model displays the features of the reactor.

30
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,040
The reactor has two main parts, the beryllium reflector and the reactor core.

31
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:46,040
This cutaway of the core vessel shows the bundle of cylindrical fuel moderator elements.

32
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,040
Each fuel element is clad in a thin-wall steel tube.

33
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:53,040
Liquid metal coolant removes the heat.

34
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:57,040
For minimum weight, the reactor uses beryllium reflectors.

35
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:03,040
The reflector is composed of two movable control drums to control the nuclear reaction

36
00:04:03,140 --> 00:04:06,040
and two safety elements for emergency shutdown.

37
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:11,040
The reactor cannot be made to sustain a nuclear reaction,

38
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:17,040
that is, be made critical, with the reflectors in the out position or with the reflectors removed.

39
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:23,040
The reflector is completely separable from the core to ensure safe reactor ground handling.

40
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:29,040
The SNAP reactor must be capable of withstanding the shock and vibrations of a missile launch.

41
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:58,040
The reactor systems must start up in orbit by remote control and be capable of unattended, reliable, and automatic operation in space for a period of at least one year.

42
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:04,040
And they must not present a radiation hazard to operating personnel or the general public.

43
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:12,040
A dominant criteria of SNAP reactor design and resulting performance is safety.

44
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:16,040
The reactor is designed for remote startup in an orbit.

45
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:20,040
It will not be operated unless a satisfactory orbit is obtained.

46
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:26,040
This reactor is one of a series of developmental reactors

47
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:30,040
built and tested to gain the necessary performance data for the flight model designs.

48
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:35,040
The use of metallic uranium, U-235,

49
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:38,040
alloyed with zirconium hydride and clad in steel,

50
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:43,040
guards against the spread of any radioactivity during storage and handling.

51
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:46,040
No shielding is required for technical personnel.

52
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:58,040
The configuration of the fuel in the core allows technicians to fabricate, assemble, and install the reactor without shielding or remote control devices.

53
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:05,040
Extensive testing of the SNAP reactor is demonstrating the inherent safety characteristics.

54
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:12,040
Critical assembly tests have measured the effect of various materials surrounding the reactor core.

55
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:24,040
The test shown here indicated the reactor can be constructed so it will not start up even if water or rocket fuel surrounds the reactor core.

56
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:36,040
Shock and vibration tests are establishing reactor resistance to accidental shock and the vibration of missile launching.

57
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:49,040
Even after acceptance tests are performed, the low amount of radioactivity generated presents no significant radiation problem.

58
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:55,040
After a short shutdown period, the reactor can be handled and disassembled for shipment.

59
00:06:56,140 --> 00:07:03,040
The design of the reactor assures against an accidental operation, or criticality, during shipment.

60
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:11,040
The reflector apparatus, including the safety and control elements, is removed and shipped separately to the launch site.

61
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:16,040
In place of the reflector assembly, a sleeve will be mounted around the reactor.

62
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:20,040
The SNAP reactor cannot operate with the sleeve surrounding the core.

63
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:27,040
The system with radiator and accessories will then be packaged in a separate shipping container.

64
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:32,040
In this manner, the reactor can be safely transported through populated areas.

65
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:38,040
Shipment can be made by truck, train, or airplane.

66
00:07:42,140 --> 00:07:48,040
The reactor cannot become critical from transportation accidents involving sudden and violent shock.

67
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:55,040
If water immersion should occur during shipment, the reactor will remain subcritical.

68
00:07:56,140 --> 00:08:00,040
And the reactor system cannot become critical in accidental fires.

69
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:10,040
Shipping procedures for nuclear material have been followed safely and satisfactorily for many years,

70
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:14,040
in accordance with Interstate Commerce Commission and other governmental regulations.

71
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:18,040
The shipment of the SNAP reactor will follow these methods.

72
00:08:22,140 --> 00:08:27,040
When the SNAP unit arrives at the launch site, it will be assembled in a controlled area.

73
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:31,040
The assembly of the reactor will be done with the control and safety elements

74
00:08:31,140 --> 00:08:34,040
mechanically safe-locked in the non-operative position.

75
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:39,040
The reactor power unit will then be transferred to a holding rack

76
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:42,040
and prepared for installation on a satellite vehicle.

77
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:51,040
This shows the mating of a non-nuclear nose cone to an Agena vehicle

78
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:54,040
before a successful Air Force launching in 1960.

79
00:08:55,140 --> 00:09:00,040
The SNAP units are designed so that established mating operations need not be changed.

80
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:07,040
Here, the Agena and the nose cone are mated to an Air Force missile.

81
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:11,040
The inherent safety of the SNAP auxiliary power unit

82
00:09:11,140 --> 00:09:15,040
will allow personnel to prepare and check out the missile and its payload.

83
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:20,040
The long-life auxiliary electric power needed by America's satellites

84
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:23,040
will be provided without altering the proven methods and procedures

85
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:25,040
of pre-launch missile preparation.

86
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:30,040
During this operation, the reactor control system is mechanically locked

87
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:32,040
in a non-operative condition.

88
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:36,040
The safety mechanism will remain on the reactor

89
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:40,040
until vehicle fueling operations require launch pad evacuation.

90
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:44,040
When personnel withdraw from the launch pad,

91
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:47,040
the mechanical safe lock is removed from the reactor.

92
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:53,040
Accidental startup is then prevented by interlocked controls in the launch control center.

93
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:58,040
During the launch of all large booster missiles,

94
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:02,040
an exclusion radius is established to protect launch site personnel

95
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:06,040
from possible fires or explosions caused by the chemical fuel.

96
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:13,040
The distance needed to ensure safety from nuclear hazards during the launch of a SNAP

97
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:19,040
reactor is less than that distance already established for missile fuel hazards.

98
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:27,040
There will be no significant hazards added to our space programs by the use of SNAP reactors.

99
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:32,040
If liquid fuel fires or explosions should cause accidental start-up of the reactor,

100
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:36,040
the nuclear operation would be momentary.

101
00:10:36,140 --> 00:10:41,040
The immediate area of the launch pad might be contaminated, but this contamination would

102
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:44,040
rapidly decay.

103
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:54,040
Cleanup would then be accomplished quickly and thoroughly by well-established procedures.

104
00:10:54,140 --> 00:11:00,040
During the flight of a vehicle carrying the SNAP reactor, the reactor will not be in operation.

105
00:11:00,140 --> 00:11:03,040
Therefore, no radioactive material will have been produced.

106
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:10,040
If the vehicle should malfunction during flight, mechanical devices will keep the reactor inoperative.

107
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:18,040
The vehicle can be safely destroyed as shown in this scene from the historical film archives of the United States Air Force.

108
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:38,040
Today missiles are rising successfully from launch pads, proving flight system reliability

109
00:11:38,140 --> 00:11:40,040
and performance refinements.

110
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:45,040
These successful launches are building the foundation in technology needed for advanced

111
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:49,040
space satellites.

112
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:56,040
Such a typical launch will carry a satellite containing the SNAP reactor into space.

113
00:11:56,140 --> 00:11:59,040
Several phases of the flight pattern will be tracked.

114
00:11:59,140 --> 00:12:03,040
Ground receiving stations will relay tracking information to central control.

115
00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:08,040
Altitude and orbit calculations will be performed.

116
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:12,040
When a safe and stable orbit has been achieved and flight geometry and orbital life have

117
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:17,040
been established, the SNAP reactor will be made critical to furnish the satellite with

118
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:22,040
the all important auxiliary power, electricity.

119
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:32,040
Then and only then will radioactive material be produced by the reactor.

120
00:12:32,140 --> 00:12:36,040
After a normal operating period of one year, the reactor will shut down and continue to

121
00:12:36,140 --> 00:12:38,040
orbit the Earth.

122
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:43,040
The initial flights of SNAP reactors will be at altitudes greater than about 700 miles,

123
00:12:43,140 --> 00:12:47,040
in orbits of at least 300 years' life.

124
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:52,040
Orbits lasting 300 years guarantee the decay of all radioactive material to safe limits prior

125
00:12:52,140 --> 00:13:00,040
to reentry. Sometime during the seventh decade of the 23rd century, the orbit of this original

126
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:09,040
but long dead reactor-powered satellite will end. Even though the intact reentry of a SNAP

127
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:16,040
reactor after 300 years in orbit results in no undue radiation hazard, the reactor is

128
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:22,040
being designed to ensure complete burn-up above 100,000 feet by means of reentry aerodynamic

129
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:25,040
heating.

130
00:13:25,140 --> 00:13:31,040
This tumbling model of a SNAP reactor fuel element is undergoing a reentry heating test.

131
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:35,040
Test conditions simulate the aerodynamic heating at various altitudes.

132
00:13:35,140 --> 00:13:39,040
The test shown here demonstrates the melting and burning that occurs at an altitude of

133
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:43,040
220,000 feet.

134
00:13:43,140 --> 00:13:48,040
These reentry heating experiments indicate that complete meltdown and burnup will occur

135
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:58,040
when a SNAP reactor enters the Earth's atmosphere from an orbit.

136
00:13:58,140 --> 00:14:03,040
Guaranteed burnup and dispersal of the reactor during reentry will permit the safe use of

137
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:09,040
SNAP reactors in our space programs at any altitude and orbit lifetime.

138
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:14,040
To facilitate exposing reactor fuel elements to aerodynamic heating, the structural materials

139
00:14:14,140 --> 00:14:20,040
surrounding the reactor are designed to separate and allow the reactor to fall apart and burn

140
00:14:20,140 --> 00:14:28,040
up on re-entry to the atmosphere.

141
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:34,040
This simulation shows a reactor satellite at an altitude over 400,000 feet, approximately

142
00:14:34,140 --> 00:14:40,040
eighty miles.

143
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:43,040
As the reactor orients ahead of the payload,

144
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:47,040
heating of the vehicle is slight.

145
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:53,040
The heating increases.

146
00:14:53,140 --> 00:15:00,040
The payload and the reactor power unit soon separate.

147
00:15:00,140 --> 00:15:09,040
At an altitude of 320,000 feet, heating of the reactor power unit is apparent and reflector

148
00:15:09,140 --> 00:15:12,040
elements are released.

149
00:15:12,140 --> 00:15:17,040
By 300,000 feet the reactor core assembly separates from the vehicle.

150
00:15:17,140 --> 00:15:20,040
The assembly begins to tumble.

151
00:15:20,140 --> 00:15:26,040
Then the fuel elements are ejected from the melting core.

152
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:31,040
At an altitude of over 100,000 feet, 20 miles above the surface of the Earth,

153
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:37,040
the ignited particles oxidize and disperse with no radiation risks to the Earth's population.

154
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:43,040
Today's systems for nuclear auxiliary power are being developed

155
00:15:43,140 --> 00:15:47,040
to furnish the needed electricity for America's space programs.

156
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:51,040
Safety is an integral part of all SNAP reactor development.

157
00:15:52,140 --> 00:15:55,040
The safety studies and safety tests are demonstrating

158
00:15:55,140 --> 00:16:00,040
the dependability of nuclear power for many remarkable and beneficial uses.

159
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:10,040
The world can confidently look forward to enjoying the maximum benefits from our national space program.

160
00:16:11,140 --> 00:16:18,040
For example, three reactor-powered satellites in equatorial orbit about 22,000 miles above the Earth

161
00:16:18,140 --> 00:16:21,040
can be used to accomplish international telecasting.

162
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:33,040
The satellites, acting as relay transmitters, can provide a television network to cover the planet and furnish worldwide communications, even to the remotest underdeveloped areas of the world.

163
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:46,040
High-altitude satellites can provide weather surveillance to furnish accurate long-range weather forecasts for agriculture and commerce.

164
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:53,040
Earth satellites can be used for navigation,

165
00:16:53,140 --> 00:16:56,040
for air traffic control,

166
00:16:56,140 --> 00:17:00,040
and for commercial radio telephone global communications.

167
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:07,040
Finally, the availability of nuclear reactors

168
00:17:07,140 --> 00:17:10,040
as a safe and dependable source for electric power

169
00:17:10,140 --> 00:17:14,040
provides mankind the dramatic and profound opportunity

170
00:17:14,140 --> 00:17:18,040
for probes into deep space and interplanetary travel.
